  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure will slowly build towards the \nregion through Wednesday. \n\nHigh pressure is expected to slowly build towards the region.\nHowever, the main mid and upper level low is still relatively\nclose - currently centered over Quebec. An embedded short wave\ntrough is expected to pivot over the region through the\nafternoon this afternoon. However, unlike yesterday, model\nsoundings show that there will be a stout mid-level inversion in\nplace during this time, so the chance for isolated showers this\nafternoon has decreased from previous forecasts.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A warm front on Thursday will bring the next \nchance for widespread rain. \n\nBy Thursday, high pressure moves offshore with southerly return\nflow behind a warm front, helping bring dew points back into \nthe low-70s. Models came in better agreement with an earlier\narrival. What that means is that showers will likely start\nmoving in, at least to portions of the region, before daybreak.\nChances for showers will continue through the day. I don't\nexpect that any one location will see rain all day, rather will\nlikely be off and on showers and storms. \n\nAs for the hazards, there is a marginal (1 out of 4) risk for \nheavy rain/flash flooding threat. Precipitable water values \nincrease to around 1.7 or 1.8 in. That is close to the 90th \npercentile for this time of year. That being said, it is lower \nthan what we have seen with some recent heavy rain events. \nAdditionally, the warm cloud layer is thinner than what we have \nseen recently (generally less than 10k ft during the afternoon).\nThus, will hold off on issuing any flood watch at this point.\n\nFor severe hazards, it will be something to monitor over the\nnext few days, but forecasted instability and shear are modest\n(CAPE values generally expected to be less than 1000 J/kg, and\nbulk shear near or below 30kt). That being said, it always bears\nwatching at this time of year, especially given the shallow\nlayer of directional (veering) shear in the lowest levels.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A cold front is expected to follow close behind\non Friday. \n\nThe timing of this cold front will be critical in the risk for \nthunderstorm hazards, and there is still differences both run-\nto-run and between models in both the timing, and if this will\nbe a single cold front, or an initial cold front with a\nreinforcing shot of cold air advection coming in behind it. The\ngood news is that most guidance is trending drier on Saturday.",
